{"id": "547-18", "kind": "sheet-level", "path": "codegen-readback", "system": "You write self-contained Python that edits a workbook with openpyxl ONLY. No torch, no network, no pip install, no os/sys/subprocess/pathlib. INIT_XLSX and OUT_XLSX are already defined as path strings. Load INIT_XLSX, compute the answer from the real workbook (not the preview), write the graded answer cells, save to OUT_XLSX. Print exactly one line: SUMMARY_JSON={\"status\": \"ok|error\", \"notes\": \"...\"}. Never fabricate numbers — compute them from the workbook. Allowed imports: openpyxl, datetime, math, json, re, statistics, collections, itertools, copy, decimal.", "prompt": "## Instruction\nI need a macro that can identify rows in Column A of the 'Imported Data' sheet where the cell content begins with two asterisks (**). Then, it should copy the entire row, starting from that cell in Column A (with the two asterisks) to the last cell with data in the same row, and paste it into the 'Summary Limits' sheet. For example, if '**BR1_NEW' is in cell A6, the macro should copy from A6 to D6 if D6 is the last cell containing data in that row, and then paste it into the 'Summary Limits' sheet. I have provided sample data with my question. Make sure that the table header is copied and pasted together.\n\n## Workbook (preview — read INIT_XLSX for full data)\n### Sheet: imported Data (showing 7x4 of 7x4)\n\tA\tB\tC\tD\n1\tName\tLimit\tType\tDescription\n2\tTOP LIMIT\t1110000\tAll\tLoan All\n3\t*PRIMARY LIMIT\t1110000\tAll\tLoan All\n4\t*RESERVE LIMIT\t0\tAll\tLoan All\n5\t*BR1 LIMIT\t1110000\tAll\tLoan All\n6\t**BR1_NEW\t1092666.6666666667\tAll\tLoan All\n7\t**BR1_USED\t17333.333333333332\tAll\tLoan All\n\n### Sheet: summary Limits (showing 1x1 of 1x1)\n\tA\n1\t\n\n### Answer range (pinned, 28 cells)\nsummary Limits!A1=\nsummary Limits!B1=\nsummary Limits!C1=\nsummary Limits!D1=\nsummary Limits!A2=\nsummary Limits!B2=\nsummary Limits!C2=\nsummary Limits!D2=\nsummary Limits!A3=\nsummary Limits!B3=\nsummary Limits!C3=\nsummary Limits!D3=\nsummary Limits!A4=\nsummary Limits!B4=\nsummary Limits!C4=\nsummary Limits!D4=\nsummary Limits!A5=\nsummary Limits!B5=\nsummary Limits!C5=\nsummary Limits!D5=\nsummary Limits!A6=\nsummary Limits!B6=\nsummary Limits!C6=\nsummary Limits!D6=\nsummary Limits!A7=\nsummary Limits!B7=\nsummary Limits!C7=\nsummary Limits!D7=\n\n## Answer range\nSheet: summary Limits\nCells: summary Limits'!A1:D7\nData range: imported Data!A1:D7','summary Limits!A1:D3'\nGraded cells: summary Limits!A1, summary Limits!B1, summary Limits!C1, summary Limits!D1, summary Limits!A2, summary Limits!B2, summary Limits!C2, summary Limits!D2, summary Limits!A3, summary Limits!B3, summary Limits!C3, summary Limits!D3, summary Limits!A4, summary Limits!B4, summary Limits!C4, summary Limits!D4, summary Limits!A5, summary Limits!B5, summary Limits!C5, summary Limits!D5, summary Limits!A6, summary Limits!B6, summary Limits!C6, summary Limits!D6, summary Limits!A7, summary Limits!B7, summary Limits!C7, summary Limits!D7\n\n\n## Skill\n### Skill: sheet reorganization\n- Never run/emit VBA or macros; implement the equivalent Python transform on the sheets.\n- When deleting rows, iterate bottom-up so indices stay valid; preserve the header row and any rows above it unless told otherwise.\n- Keep sheet names byte-exact (quotes/spaces matter). Do not create helper sheets or write outside the requested target range.\n\n### Skill: writing cells\n- Write computed VALUES (not formulas) unless the task explicitly requires formula text.\n- Write every requested answer cell; an unwritten cell scores zero. Convert Python types to plain scalars (no numpy types, no datetime objects — use ISO strings).\n\n\nReply with a single Python script in a ```python fence. No prose. INIT_XLSX and OUT_XLSX are predefined. Save OUT_XLSX and print one SUMMARY_JSON line.", "completion": "{\"cells\": [{\"cell\": \"A1\", \"value\": \"Name\", \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"B1\", \"value\": \"Limit\", \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"C1\", \"value\": \"Type\", \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"D1\", \"value\": \"Description\", \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"A2\", \"value\": \"**BR1_NEW\", \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"B2\", \"value\": 1092666.666666667, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"C2\", \"value\": \"All\", \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"D2\", \"value\": \"Loan All\", \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"A3\", \"value\": \"**BR1_USED\", \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"B3\", \"value\": 17333.33333333333, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"C3\", \"value\": \"All\", \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"D3\", \"value\": \"Loan All\", \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"A4\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"B4\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"C4\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"D4\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"A5\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"B5\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"C5\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"D5\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"A6\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"B6\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"C6\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"D6\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"A7\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"B7\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"C7\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}, {\"cell\": \"D7\", \"value\": null, \"sheet\": \"summary Limits\"}]}", "sample_idx": 4, "latency_s": 11.3, "in_tokens": 1109, "out_tokens": 485}
{"id": "61-4", "kind": "sheet-level", "path": "codegen-readback", "system": "You write self-contained Python that edits a workbook with openpyxl ONLY. No torch, no network, no pip install, no os/sys/subprocess/pathlib. INIT_XLSX and OUT_XLSX are already defined as path strings. Load INIT_XLSX, compute the answer from the real workbook (not the preview), write the graded answer cells, save to OUT_XLSX. Print exactly one line: SUMMARY_JSON={\"status\": \"ok|error\", \"notes\": \"...\"}. Never fabricate numbers — compute them from the workbook. Allowed imports: openpyxl, datetime, math, json, re, statistics, collections, itertools, copy, decimal.", "prompt": "## Instruction\nHow can I identify consecutive negative values in a column for specific stock names, and then merge the related data such that only the opening price from the first date, the closing price from the last date, the highest 'high' price, the lowest 'low' price within those dates are kept, and the volume from these dates is summed? This summarized data should then be sent to an output sheet. The input and output formats are illustrated in an attached Excel document, with color coding for clarity.Remarks : please chek comments inside cell C2,D2,E2,F2,G2.For better understanding, I kept the output to column K:Q,but this data will be shifted to \"Output sheet\".\n\n## Workbook (preview — read INIT_XLSX for full data)\n### Sheet: input (showing 21x18 of 21x18)\n\tA\tB\tC\tD\tE\tF\tG\tH\tI\tJ\tK\tL\tM\tN\tO\tP\tQ\tR\n1\tDATE \tStock Name\tOPENP* \tHIGH \tLOW \tCLOSEP* \tVOLUME\tChange\t\t\tDATE \tStock Name\tOPENP* \tHIGH \tLOW \tCLOSEP* \tVOLUME\t\n2\t2023-09-17 00:00:00\tAAMRANET \t58\t58.5\t56.6\t57.1\t554558\t-1.1999999999999957\t\t\t2023-09-17 00:00:00\tAAMRANET \t59.6\t59.6\t56.6\t57.1\t1521647\t\n3\t2023-09-14 00:00:00\tAAMRANET \t58.4\t59.5\t58.1\t58.3\t327685\t-0.4000000000000057\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n4\t2023-09-13 00:00:00\tAAMRANET \t59.6\t59.6\t58\t58.7\t639404\t-1\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n5\t2023-09